<<~ ahu slot=`#/contract`>>

!! Contract

`IslandAdaptor` implements `MemeProjection` and stands as the TS shore between the composite store and the wiki's nalu engine. The law it enacts lives at `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/outbound-bridge#/the-shore-law`; this carrier gives the file's own account.

''Inbound (projection).'' It queues each `LarTiddlerChange` in its landing queue and lands it, slice by slice, as the composition resolves its title, handing each result to `$tw.lares.applyNalu()`. Initial replay flows the same road: `emitInitialReplay()` fires one change per existing record, the fan-out reaches `onUriChanged`, and the seed checkpoint resolves once the queue and its tracked work settle.

''Outbound (TW5 edits).'' The outbound bridge (`packages/lararium-tw5/src/outbound-bridge.ts`) reads each live wiki `change` dispatch and hands every hand to `saveTiddler` / `deleteTiddler`; they pace per title, route through the bag-path cascade to `store.writeFamily` at or above the working layer, or tombstone in the bag the composition's layer order picks. A saved tiddler's fields string through TW5's own `Tiddler.getFieldStrings()` (`toTW5FieldStrings`), so a date and a list take TW5's own spelling, an absent field stays out, and no second normaliser stands beside it.

''The composition read stays in TS,'' because it needs an async `resolveLayered` on the composite.

''The echo law reads what stands.'' The adaptor keeps a landed ledger: the value each wave left under each title. A `change` whose title holds that value reads as foam; any other reads as a hand. `$tw.lares.isApplyingNalu()` serves a synchronous caller alone, since TW5 dispatches `change` on `nextTick`, after an apply lowered that flag.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/what-the-wiki-owns`>>

!! What the Nalu Engine Owns Instead

The TW5 module `nalu-engine` holds the one piece the adaptor deliberately does not: the apply. `applyNalu(change)` lands ONE change in the wiki synchronously, and `isApplyingNalu()` reads true while it runs. The queue, its slices, the pacing between them and the seed checkpoint all stand on the adaptor's side.

''One writer, one place.'' The shore decides WHEN a change lands; the engine decides HOW. Splitting the queue across the shore and the wiki would give two owners one queue.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-cascade`>>

!! The Bag-Path Cascade

`routeBag(wiki, title)` (`bag-cascade.ts`) walks `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/config/bag-paths` — newline-separated filter expressions evaluated against a single-tiddler source, first non-empty result winning. It mirrors TW5's own `$:/config/FileSystemPaths` shape, and takes the shape of `$:/config/SyncFilter` too: the shipped cascade opens with the exclusions and ends in a catch-all.

''The cascade routes; it does not decide whether to write.'' Everything a hand edits and saves travels through the composition and round-trips. Only a rule that NAMES what it withholds may withhold a write. Two things stand outside that sentence: `neverPersists` withholds the volatile VM plane and the alert rail ahead of the cascade, and the temp slot's store lives in memory on the device.

''Three outcomes, never one.'' A rule may ROUTE a title, a rule may WITHHOLD it, or no rule may reach it at all — and the third names a ROUTER GAP rather than a decision. Read as one, a gap drops the write in silence: the promise resolves, the wiki shows the edit, nothing persists, and the tiddler is gone on the next boot. The walk reports which of the three happened, and each caller acts differently on each.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/slot-of`>>

!! The Landed Ledger

This section, anchored `#/slot-of`, holds the landed ledger: what the shore records per title and which acts read it.

The adaptor's landed ledger (`packages/lararium-tw5/src/landed-ledger.ts`) records, per title, what the last wave left: the wiki value, the bag it landed from and that bag's heads. Every landing writes it; nothing else does.

''A delete reads it BEFORE the cascade,'' because by then the tiddler has left the wiki and no field answers (the bag of the adaptor's own last acknowledged write of the title, where one stands, answers ahead of the ledger). That ordering lets a draft loaded from the CRDT at boot, and deleted before any save, resolve to its bag. The composition's layer order then picks where the tombstone lands (`_tombstoneBag`).

''A notification reads against it,'' so a wave never re-saves and a hand always does.

The ledger stays volatile: one adaptor, one session. A stopped adaptor clears it.

<<~/ahu>>

